+# Hegel Property-Based Testing Report
+
+Property-based tests were added with `hegeltest` 0.28.2 (dev-dependency, added with
+`cargo add --dev hegeltest -p <crate> --ignore-rust-version` because the workspace pins
+`rust-version = "1.85"` while hegeltest requires 1.86) to three plumbing crates whose
+`fuzz/` targets only cover parse-robustness (no roundtrip/consistency assertions):
+`gix-url`, `gix-refspec`, and `gix-config`.
+
+All tests live in the crates' existing integration-test files, next to the unit tests they
+generalize. `.hegel/` was added to the root `.gitignore`.
+
+## Properties tested
+
+### gix-url (`gix-url/tests/url/parse/mod.rs`, mod `properties`)
+
+1. **`parsed_urls_roundtrip_through_to_bstring`** — for any input (arbitrary bytes, arbitrary
+   text, structured `scheme://[user[:pass]@]host[:port]/path` URLs including percent-escapes,
+   SCP-like targets) that `gix_url::parse()` accepts, `parse(url.to_bstring())` succeeds and
+   equals the original `Url`.
+   *Evidence:* `Url::write_to()` docs: "Write this URL losslessly to `out`, ready to be parsed
+   again"; the existing `assert_url_roundtrip()` helper checks exactly this for ~30 fixed examples.
+2. **`from_parts_preserves_components`** — `Url::from_parts()` built from valid components
+   (scheme in {http, https, ssh, git}, unicode user/password, host incl. IPv4/IPv6, port 1–65535,
+   `/`-prefixed path) preserves every component (modulo documented normalizations: lowercase
+   hosts, `/~` stripping for ssh/git, IPv6 bracket storage forms).
+   *Evidence:* field docs on `Url` ("Stored in decoded form... Re-encoded during canonical
+   serialization", the IPv6 bracket rules, the `/~` path normalization), and `from_parts()`
+   validating "by parsing them back".
+3. **`users_with_colons_roundtrip_through_to_bstring`** — KNOWN FAILURE, pins bug #1 below.
+
+### gix-refspec (`tests/refspec/write.rs`, `impls.rs`, `spec.rs`, mods `properties`)
+
+4. **`parsed_specs_reproduce_in_parseable_form`** — any refspec accepted by
+   `gix_refspec::parse()` (for both `Operation::Fetch` and `Operation::Push`, from arbitrary
+   text/bytes and structured `[+^]src[:dst]` shapes) serializes via `to_bstring()` to something
+   that reparses under the same operation with an equal `instruction()`.
+   *Evidence:* `RefSpecRef::to_bstring()` doc: "Reproduce ourselves in parseable form";
+   `PartialEq` for specs is defined via `instruction()`.
+5. **`one_sided_push_glob_specs_reproduce_in_parseable_form`** — KNOWN FAILURE, pins bug #2 below.
+6. **`to_owned_to_ref_is_identity`** — `spec.to_owned().to_ref()` equals the original spec and
+   serializes identically.
+   *Evidence:* `to_owned()` doc: "Convert this ref into a standalone, owned copy".
+7. **`prefix_is_a_prefix_of_the_relevant_side`** — whenever `prefix()` returns `Some(p)`, `p` is
+   a string prefix of the relevant side (source for fetch, destination for push).
+   *Evidence:* `prefix()` docs ("Exact refs starting with `refs/` are returned unchanged...
+   glob refspecs return the portion up to their single `*`") and the example-based tests in
+   `tests/refspec/spec.rs::prefix`.
+
+### gix-config (`tests/config/file/write.rs`, `tests/config/file/access/raw/set_raw_value.rs`)
+
+8. **`serialization_is_a_fixed_point`** — for any parseable input (a `config_ish` generator
+   producing sections/subsections in both quoted and dotted form, values with escapes, quotes,
+   line continuations, trailing comments, `\n`/`\r\n` newline styles, plus raw text/bytes
+   branches), `file.to_bstring()` parses again, and serializing the reparse is byte-identical.
+   *Evidence:* `write_to()` doc "reproduce this file mostly losslessly as it was parsed"
+   (normalizations happen only on the *first* cycle, see `inserted_newlines_use_each_sections_
+   newline_style`); the crate's own fuzz target reparses `to_bstring()` output.
+9. **`roundtrip_preserves_sections_and_values`** — a parse/serialize cycle preserves the ordered
+   list of sections (name, subsection) and each section's (value-name, value) pairs.
+   *Evidence:* generalization of the existing `complex_lossless_roundtrip` test.
+10. **`subsection_names_roundtrip_through_serialization`** — a value stored under an arbitrary
+    valid subsection name (anything without `\n`/`\0`, boosted with `"`/`\`/brackets/whitespace)
+    is retrievable under the same name after a serialize/parse cycle.
+    *Evidence:* `is_valid_subsection()`, `write_escaped_subsection()`, and the existing
+    `decoded_subsection_names_keep_their_raw_spelling` test.
+11. **`set_value_can_be_read_back_after_serialization`** — `set_raw_value_by()` with arbitrary
+    byte values (binary and unicode) reads back identically via `raw_value_by()`, both
+    immediately and after a serialize/parse cycle. This evolves the hand-picked
+    `assert_set_value()` examples in the same file into a property.
+    *Evidence:* `set_raw_value()` doctests assert exactly this for fixed values; `escape_value()`
+    escapes `\n`, `\t`, `"`, `\` on write and `value::normalize()` decodes on read.
+
+That is 9 distinct properties plus 2 deterministic known-failure pins.
+
+## Test results
+
+- Liveness verified: every property was observed reporting a shrunk counterexample at least once
+  during development; for gix-config (whose final properties never failed naturally) an assertion
+  was temporarily inverted and hegel reported a minimal counterexample
+  (`section="a", key="a", value=[]`), then reverted.
+- Exploratory 10x run (`test_cases = 1000` on all 11 tests, then reverted to the default):
+  identical outcome to the default run — only the two KNOWN FAILURE tests fail.
+- Final full runs:
+  - `cargo test -p gix-config`: **all green** (392 tests passed, incl. 4 new properties).
+  - `cargo test -p gix-url`: 114 passed, **1 failed**: `users_with_colons_roundtrip_through_to_bstring`
+    (intentional, pins bug #1).
+  - `cargo test -p gix-refspec`: 78 passed, **1 failed**: `one_sided_push_glob_specs_reproduce_in_parseable_form`
+    (intentional, pins bug #2).
+  Both failing tests fail deterministically on every run: their generators only produce inputs
+  inside the buggy region, so every one of the 100 cases fails and hegel shrinks to the minimal
+  counterexample each time.
+
+## Suspected bugs
+
+### 1. gix-url: `Url::to_bstring()` is lossy for users containing `:` (silent user/password corruption)
+
+- **Minimal counterexample** (shrunk by hegel): `parse("http://%3A@host/")` →
+  `Url { user: Some(":"), password: None, .. }` → `to_bstring()` = `"http://:@host/"` →
+  reparses as `Url { user: None, password: None, .. }`. Non-degenerate form:
+  `parse("http://a%3Ab@host/")` → user `a:b` → serializes `http://a:b@host/` → reparses as
+  user `a`, password `b`.
+- **Why it's a library bug:** `Url::user` is documented as "Stored in decoded form...
+  Re-encoded during canonical serialization", and `write_to()` promises "Write this URL
+  losslessly to `out`, ready to be parsed again". A colon inside the user component must be
+  percent-encoded because the first unencoded `:` in userinfo delimits user from password —
+  round-tripping turns part of a username into a password, which the crate's own security notes
+  treat as sensitive. `Url::from_parts(user: Some("a:b"), ..)` also silently returns a `Url`
+  with user `a` and password `b`.
+- **Code path:** `write_canonical_form_to()` in `gix-url/src/lib.rs` — `USERINFO_ENCODE_SET`
+  contains `@`, `/`, `[`, `]` etc. but not `:`; the user is written with
+  `percent_encode(user)` and the reparse splits userinfo at the first `:`
+  (`simple_url.rs::parse`, `rsplit_once('@')` then `split_once(':')`).
+- **Pinned by:** `gix-url/tests/url/parse/mod.rs::properties::users_with_colons_roundtrip_through_to_bstring`.
+  Excluded (with comments referencing the canonical test) from the two sibling properties.
+
+### 2. gix-refspec: one-sided push specs with complex globs serialize to an unparseable form
+
+- **Minimal counterexample** (shrunk by hegel): `parse("**", Operation::Push)` succeeds; its
+  `instruction()` is `Push::Matching { src: "**", dst: "**" }`; `to_bstring()` yields `"**:**"`,
+  which fails to reparse with `PatternUnsupported { pattern: "**" }`. Also found with invalid
+  ref-name bytes: `parse("\0*", Push)` → `to_bstring()` = `"\0*:\0*"` → `InvalidByte` on reparse.
+- **Why it's a library bug:** `to_bstring()` documents "Reproduce ourselves in parseable form".
+  The parser intentionally relaxes validation for one-sided specs (`validated()` in
+  `gix-refspec/src/parse.rs` skips *all* validation when `is_one_sided && has_globs`, per the
+  intentional behavior change noted in `tests/refspec/parse/mod.rs::baseline`), but
+  `RefSpecRef::instruction()` maps a one-sided push spec to `Push::Matching { src, dst: src }`,
+  whose serialization `src:src` is a *two-sided* spec subject to the strict validation
+  (single `*`, valid ref-name bytes). Anything accepted only thanks to the one-sided relaxation
+  therefore cannot round-trip; consumers persisting `spec.to_bstring()` (the advertised way to
+  re-store a spec) write configuration they can no longer read.
+- **Code path:** `gix-refspec/src/parse.rs::validated()` (one-sided glob bypass) +
+  `gix-refspec/src/spec.rs::instruction()` (`(Normal|Force, Some(src), None)` →
+  `Push::Matching { src, dst: src }`) + `gix-refspec/src/write.rs` (writes `src:dst`).
+- **Pinned by:** `gix-refspec/tests/refspec/write.rs::properties::one_sided_push_glob_specs_reproduce_in_parseable_form`.
+  Excluded (with a comment referencing the canonical test) from the sibling roundtrip property.
+
+### Minor observation (not pinned, arguably doc-vs-behavior)
+
+`gix-url`'s `Url::host` docs say IPv6 hosts are stored *with* brackets for non-SSH schemes, but
+brackets are only added during serialization when a port is present (`needs_brackets` in
+`write_canonical_form_to`). So `from_parts(Http, host: Some("::1"), port: None, ..)` yields a
+`Url` whose host is stored `"::1"` (serializes as the non-RFC form `http://::1/`), while the
+same call with a port stores `"[::1]"`. The representation of the same host thus depends on the
+presence of a port. The roundtrip property still holds in both cases, so this was handled by
+generating the documented storage form (bracketed for non-SSH) rather than pinned as a failing
+test; it may be worth a maintainer's attention.
+
+## Unsound properties abandoned
+
+- **`from_parts` preserves `Some("")` user/password** — abandoned. `http://@host/` and
+  `http://u:@host/` reparse with `None` user/password; empty userinfo components are degenerate
+  values that essentially every URL implementation treats as absent, and the field docs describe
+  the user as "the user to impersonate", which an empty string is not. Generators now require
+  non-empty user/password (documented in comments).
+- **`from_parts` preserves port `0`** — not asserted; port 0 is explicitly outside the domain
+  (existing test `invalid::port_zero_is_invalid`, error "invalid port number - must be between
+  1-65535"). Generator constrained to 1–65535 with a comment.
+- **Byte-exact `to_bstring() == input` for gix-config** — deliberately *not* asserted for the
+  first parse/serialize cycle. `write_to()` promises only "mostly losslessly": the existing
+  `inserted_newlines_use_each_sections_newline_style` test shows a documented normalization
+  (e.g. `[b]y=2` gains a newline after the header). The property was formulated as a fixed point
+  after one cycle instead, which is the strongest sound version and did hold.
+- An earlier formulation of the **refspec `prefix()` property** rejected too many inputs
+  (hegel's `FilterTooMuch` health check fired because `prefix()` is `None` for most arbitrary
+  specs). It was reworked with a generator biased toward `refs/`-prefixed sides rather than by
+  suppressing the health check.
+
+## Skill feedback
+
+Overall the skill worked well; both real bugs were found by the workflow it prescribes
+(roundtrip properties on parse/serialize pairs, reading the crate's `fuzz/` directory to see
+what is *not* covered, broad generators first, investigate on failure). Specific notes:
+
+1. **The MSRV gotcha in the Rust reference was exactly right.** `cargo add --dev hegeltest`
+   failed with the predicted resolution error and `--ignore-rust-version` fixed it. Having that
+   in the Setup section saved real time.
+2. **The "boundary conjunctions need help" advice proved itself.** The colon-user bug was
+   invisible to `text()`-based generation at default *and* 10x counts (it needs `%3A` or a raw
+   `:` inside userinfo in an otherwise-valid URL). It was found only by reading the encode-set
+   in the source and then targeting the suspicious gap. The skill covers this ("read the code
+   for suspicious arithmetic... and target the conjunction it implies") — it may be worth
+   stating for *character sets* too, e.g. "when a serializer has an explicit encode-set,
+   generate characters just outside it".
+3. **`FilterTooMuch` guidance worked as designed** — the health-check failure message plus the
+   skill's "restructure generators to produce valid inputs directly" led to the biased
+   `prefixy_side()` generator instead of suppressing the check.
+4. **Known-failure pinning guidance was actionable** (boost the generator to the failing region
+   so the test fails every run; exclude the region from siblings with a comment referencing the
+   canonical test). Both pinned tests fail on 100/100 cases and shrink to minimal
+   counterexamples on every run.
+5. **Minor reference gap:** the composite/closure interplay — `#[hegel::composite]` functions
+   cannot be *returned* as values from other generators, and helper closures taking
+   `&hegel::TestCase` (documented) were the right tool inside composites; an example of a
+   composite that internally uses per-branch helper closures would have matched real usage here.
+6. **Minor reference gap:** counterexample output labels draws from inside `one_of!`/composites
+   as `draw_1`, `host_1` etc. — the reference mentions unnamed draws for helpers, but not that
+   `one_of!` branches inside composites show the composite parameter name with a suffix.
+   Harmless, but knowing what output to expect would help when reading failures.
+7. **One place the skill's file-placement rule needed interpretation:** "add tests to the
+   appropriate existing test file" worked (properties were placed beside the unit tests they
+   generalize in `write.rs`, `spec.rs`, `impls.rs`, `set_raw_value.rs`), but sharing one
+   generator across several existing files required making the generator module `pub(crate)`
+   inside one of them, which felt slightly against the grain; a sentence blessing a shared
+   `pub(crate)` generator inside one of the existing files would remove the hesitation.
